= 1
{parent=Paper 75}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <incompressibility>, negligible <viscosity> and <surface tension>, and an initially <irrotational flow>. Take the undisturbed <free surface> at $z=0$ and bed at $z=-H$. The <velocity potential> $\varphi$ satisfies <Laplace equation> and $\varphi_z(-H)=0$. For a <Fourier mode>, write
$$
\varphi=A\cosh[k(z+H)]e^{i(kx-\omega t)},\qquad \eta=\eta_a e^{i(kx-\omega t)},\qquad k>0.
$$
The linear <kinematic boundary condition> and linear <Bernoulli equation> at the <free surface> are $\eta_t=\varphi_z$ and $\varphi_t+g\eta=0$. Eliminating $A,\eta_a$ gives the <surface gravity wave> <dispersion relation>
$$
\boxed{\omega^2=gk\tanh(kH)}.
$$
The rightward <phase velocity> and <group velocity> are
$$
c_p=\frac\omega k=\sqrt{\frac gk\tanh(kH)},\qquad c_g=\frac{d\omega}{dk}=\frac{c_p}{2}\left[1+\frac{2kH}{\sinh(2kH)}\right].
$$
The <phase velocity> transports a crest; the <group velocity> transports a narrow-band envelope and its leading-order <energy>. For $kH\gg1$, $\omega\sim\sqrt{gk}$ and $c_g\sim c_p/2\sim\sqrt{g/k}/2$: deep-water <surface gravity waves> are dispersive. For $kH\ll1$, $\omega\sim k\sqrt{gH}$ and $c_g\sim c_p\sim\sqrt{gH}$, the nondispersive <shallow-water dispersion relation>. The boundary <velocity> controls the excited amplitude; it does not change the linear <dispersion relation>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Before <surface-wave breaking> or reflected waves return, the <shallow water equations> are $h_t+(hu)_x=0$ and $u_t+uu_x+gh_x=0$. Set $c=\sqrt{gh}$ and $c_0=\sqrt{gH}$. Their <Riemann invariants> are $u\pm2c$ along $dx/dt=u\pm c$. An outgoing <simple wave> entering resting water has $u-2c=-2c_0$, so the imposed boundary <velocity> gives
$$
\boxed{h(0,t)=\frac1g\left[c_0+\frac U2\sin(\omega t)\right]^2}.
$$
This formula requires $c_0+u/2>0$ and an appropriate inflow/outflow <characteristic curve> count. In particular $U<2c_0/3$ ensures that only the outgoing family enters the domain throughout the cycle; stronger forcing can change the boundary problem.

A <characteristic curve> emitted at time $s$ carries constant $u=U\sin\omega s$ and $h=h(0,s)$ and follows
$$
x=v(s)(t-s),\qquad v(s)=c_0+\frac32U\sin(\omega s).
$$
Increasing boundary <velocity> produces faster following <characteristic curves>, steepening the advancing side of a crest; decreasing boundary <velocity> spreads the curves and produces a <rarefaction wave>. A trough has reduced depth and smaller propagation speed. Once the compressive curves intersect, the single-valued <simple wave> must be replaced by an admissible <hydraulic bore>, with <mass conservation> and <momentum conservation> across it and loss of mechanical <energy>. In a very weak or dispersive wavetrain an undular bore can replace the ideal discontinuity.

For a bore of speed $s_b$ entering undisturbed depth $H$, the <Rankine-Hugoniot condition> gives $s_b[h]=[hu]$ and $s_b[hu]=[hu^2+gh^2/2]$. If $h_2>H$ is its depth just behind, elimination of the behind-bore <velocity> yields $s_b^2=gh_2(h_2+H)/(2H)$ and $u_2=s_b(h_2-H)/h_2$. Thus the newly formed front travels faster than $c_0$, carries a depth jump, and dissipates <energy>; the behind-bore state must satisfy these balances rather than continuing intersecting smooth curves. The final sketch is schematic and does not claim a solved post-breaking boundary-value problem.

There is a numerical defect in the printed leading-edge claim. The first leading edge is the $s=0$ curve $x=c_0t$. The envelope condition $\partial_s x=0$ gives
$$
t=s+\frac{v(s)}{v'(s)},\qquad x_b(s)=\frac{v(s)^2}{v'(s)}.
$$
At the leading edge,
$$
\boxed{t_{\rm lead}=\frac{2c_0}{3U\omega},\qquad x_{\rm lead}=\frac{2gH}{3U\omega}}.
$$
This follows directly from the specified boundary acceleration and the exact <Riemann invariant>. The printed coefficient $4/9$ is incompatible with these data: at that distance the leading-edge <characteristic curves> have not yet intersected. For the first compression interval, $0<\omega s<\pi/2$, one has $v''<0$. Consequently $d(s+v/v')/ds=2-vv''/v'^2>0$, so the first envelope occurs at $s=0$. This <boundary-driven shallow-water wave steepening> calculation also establishes that no later characteristic in the initial compression forms a bore earlier.

\Image[paper-75-wave-steepening.png]
{title=Outgoing shallow-water characteristics and profiles before the leading-edge bore}

